Network intrusion detection systems (NIDS) in production must detect attack families never seen in training, tolerate benign traffic drift, and adapt with the few labels that analysts can provide. Detectors that adapt by absorbing the traffic they accept can be steered by slow poisoning, while continual learners that avoid this depend on hundreds of labels per day. We propose \ERNP (Evidence-Retracted Novelty Prior), a detector whose only adaptation channel is the retraction of a structured novelty prior where analysts have confirmed benign traffic. \ERNP estimates a density ratio between benign evidence and the union of attack evidence and a prior built by partially swapping features between benign flows; unlabelled traffic never enters the model. We prove that retraction is local and bounded by analyst errors, and that detection far from confirmed evidence is preserved up to a computable factor. On four temporally split benchmarks, five seeds and a causal protocol, \ERNP has the best mean rank against five recent detectors (SSF, OWAD, CADE, Trident, MemStream), with mean F1 0.867 against 0.671 for the strongest. With 25 labels per day it outperforms SSF using 200--400, and a counterfactual that feeds \ERNP its own verdicts instead of analyst evidence collapses from 0.74 to 0.07 zero-day payload recall under controlled poisoning. \ERNP does not win everywhere: it is on par with CADE on unsampled CIC-IDS2017 and degrades when analysts mislabel 10\% or more of the queried alert groups.
